## Abstract

This project is a methods study. We **design** a known 20-node risk-propagation network `W_true` across 20 Principal Risk Types — documenting a source for each of its ~40 directed edges, informed by supervisory stress-testing frameworks and operational-risk literature — and use it to generate 100 synthetic Key Risk Indicator (KRI) time series (five per PRT) of 250 monthly observations. `W_true` enters both the VAR(1) lag matrix and the contemporaneous copula correlation matrix `Σ = (I − ρW_true)^{−1} D (I − ρW_true)^{−T}`; the lag-matrix channel is what makes edge direction identifiable by lag-based estimators. We benchmark **three recovery estimators** — VAR with Diebold–Yılmaz connectedness, CoVaR (Adrian & Brunnermeier), and a Graph Neural Network — **plus one propagation operator**, DebtRank (Battiston et al.), under varying data volume, noise, tail dependence, and network density. DebtRank consumes a supplied adjacency and returns cascade severity; it is **excluded from the recovery metrics and evaluated only on impulse-response MSE**, because precision, recall, F1, and AUC-ROC are undefined for it. To ensure results do not hinge on our network design, every configuration is re-run on **random sparse networks of the same density**, redrawn per replication. Performance is measured with a base-rate-aware four-metric rule — AUC-ROC, AUPRC, F1 at the oracle threshold, and F1 at a calibrated threshold — alongside edge-weight correlation and impulse-response MSE. We do not claim to discover a real risk network operating inside African banks; our conclusions concern which estimators recover interconnectedness best, and under what conditions. A propagation sanity check — a single +0.30 shock to Technology_Risk cascading through Cyber, Operational, Reputational, Liquidity, External Fraud, and Credit over 13 periods — confirms that the synthetic environment produces a plausible cascade before any estimator is benchmarked against it. The deliverable is a reproducible benchmarking framework and an empirical map of estimator performance, not a production platform.

**Keywords:** Risk contagion; Network recovery; Key Risk Indicators; Primary Risk Types; Student's t-copula; Diebold–Yılmaz connectedness; CoVaR; Graph Neural Networks; Synthetic data benchmarking.

**JEL classification:** G21, C45, C58

**Code and data:** https://github.com/eodenyire/mscfe-690-risk-contagion-methods-study

